{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "Variables in C#"}, {"type": "position", "content": 7}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 3}, {"type": "level", "content": "B1.1"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you hear and use when talking about variables in C#."}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Talk about principles, concepts, characteristics of general-purpose computer programming language in spoken and written situations."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Discusses a range of options for potential actions at work through the correct use of grammar, vocabulary; level of formality and purpose, and cohesion and coherence aspects appropriate to this level.   Exchanges simple information on principles, concepts, and features of general purpose computer programming languages."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe7n5le03ob07re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Look for more information about variables in C#."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Describe every type of value types in detail."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Make a poster with those descriptions."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Make a presentation about how the syntax of C# works and check if your partners understood. "}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I look for more information about variables in C#."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I describe every type of value in detail"}, {"type": "text", "content": "I make a poster with the descriptions and make a presentation about C# syntax for my partners to understand the topic."}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Present Continuous and Simple Present to Talk About Upcoming Events"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "<span class=\"strong\">Present continuous tense</span>  and <span class=\"strong\">simple present tense</span> are commonly used to talk about events or situations that take place in the present. However, we can also use these tenses to describe <span class=\"underline\">future events</span>. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "The engineers are coming this week, right?"}, {"type": "example", "content": "In fact, I deliver a training session about it next week."}, {"type": "text", "content": "The <b>present continuous </b>can have a future meaning. This is used for future arrangements. </br></br> Look at the following structure: <br><br> <center> Subject + <b>verb to be ( am/is/are) + verb in gerund ( -ing )</b> + complement  </center><br><br>    Example: <br><span class=\"italic\">We <span class=\"strong\">are reading</span> about variables next Monday.</span></br></br>   The <b>present simple</b> can also have a future meaning. We use it in two situations: </br></br>  -Timetabled events </br> -Sentence with future meaning</br></br>   In order to write future statements, we follow the usual structure of the present simple. However, we have to specify the time when the action will happen. <br><br>    Examples: </br> <span class=\"italic\">The class on variables <span class=\"strong\">starts</span> at 11:00. </span></br> <span class=\"italic\">We <span class=\"strong\">begin</span> with variables after we finish this topic. </span></br>"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Basic Value Types Provided in C#"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "There are different categories of <b>value types in C#</b>. Take a look at the following chart in which there are examples of each type."}, {"type": "table", "content": "{\"rows\":6,\"columns\":2,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<div class=\\\"center_text\\\"><span class=\\\"strong\\\">Type</span></div>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<div class=\\\"center_text\\\"><span class=\\\"strong\\\">Example</span></div>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\" Integral types\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\" Sbyte, byte, short, ushort, int, uint, long, ulong      \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\" Floating point types\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\" Float and double\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\" Decimal types  \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\" Decimal\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\" Boolean types \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"True or false values, as assigned \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\" Nullable types \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\" Nullable data types\\\"}\"],\"styles\":\" instruction_table_background intro_table_border\"}"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "compulsory "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "obligatory"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example:You'd better explain to me why everybody except you is attending the compulsory training sessions this week."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le03ob07aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": " deliver "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to give"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: I deliver a training session about it next week."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le03ob07aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": " need a hand "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to need help "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(expression)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example:  If they need a hand with anything, I am available after 3:00 p.m. every day."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le03ob07aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": " drop by "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to come "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(phrasal verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: You ought to drop by my office this week."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le03ob07aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "department"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "an area of special expertise in an organization"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: We can see if there are any positions in the programming department more suitable for your talents. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le03ob07aud06"}], "type": "target_word"}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"stimuli": [{"template_override": 1, "type": "welcome", "_data": [], "contents": [{}], "id": 0}, {"template_override": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"images": [{"time": "0", "image": "enpe7n5le03ob07re2img01.jpg", "id": 0, "text": "<span class=\"strong\">Manager:</span> Baxter! You'd better explain to me why everybody except you is attending the compulsory training sessions this week.</br>\n<span class=\"strong\">Baxter: </span> Sorry. It's just that I learned all about concept variables in C# in my last job. In fact, I deliver a training session about it next week.</br>\n<span class=\"strong\">Manager: </span>  Oh, really? Well, if you're so smart, you had better tell me what variables in C# are exactly? </br>\n<span class=\"strong\">Baxter: </span> Well, variables in this programming language are storage areas that our programs can manipulate. They have a specific type which determines how they will store data. </br>"}, {"time": "37000", "image": "enpe7n5le03ob07re2img02.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "<span class=\"strong\">Manager: </span>  Okay, Baxter. What about the syntax? </br>\n<span class=\"strong\">Baxter: </span>  Let me write this on the board: <span class=\"strong\">  < data_type >  </span> must be a valid C# data type such as <span class=\"strong\">char, int, float, double</span>, or any user-defined data type. Look!  <span class=\"strong\">  < variable_list > </span>  may consist of one or more identifier names separated by commas. Some valid variable definitions are \u201c<span class=\"strong\">int</span>  i, j, k; <span class=\"strong\">char</span>  c, ch; <span class=\"strong\">float</span>  f, salary; <span class=\"strong\"> double</span>  d;\u201d </br>\n<span class=\"strong\">Manager: </span>  Baxter, you weren't lying. What about the syntax for initializing variables? </br>\n<span class=\"strong\">Baxter: </span>  Like I said, I'm actually an expert in C#. You initialize a variable using an equal sign followed by a constant expression. The general form of initialization is: <span class=\"strong\">  variable_name = value;. </span>  Variables can be initialized in their declaration. The initializer consists of an equal sign followed by a constant expression as: <span class=\"strong\"> < data_type > </span><span class=\"strong\"> < variable_name > </span< class=\"strong\">= value; </span>  </br>"}, {"time": "137600", "image": "enpe7n5le03ob07re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "<span class=\"strong\">Manager: </span>  Seriously, I'm lost for words; you really know your stuff. </br>\n<span class=\"strong\">Baxter: </span>  Thanks. The engineers are coming this week, right? If they need a hand with anything, I am available after 3:00 p.m. every day. </br>\n<span class=\"strong\"> Manager: </span>  You ought to drop by my office this week, and we can see if there are any positions in the programming department more suitable for your talents. </br>\n<span class=\"strong\"> Baxter: </span>  That'd be great. </br>"}], "coverimg": "enpe7n5le03ob07re2img01.jpg", "audiosrc": "enpe7n5le03ob07re2aud01"}, "type": "slideshow"}], "type": "audio", "_d